# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and on checkout, on every platform.
#
# This is not cosmetic. `docker/entrypoint.sh` runs inside a Linux container: if Git checks it
# out with CRLF endings on a Windows clone, the shebang becomes `#!/bin/sh\r` and the container
# dies with a baffling "no such file or directory". Anyone who clones this repo on Windows and
# runs `docker compose up` would hit that, which is exactly the adoption path that has to work.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out per type as well as by the wildcard above. Redundant by design: if someone
# loosens the `*` rule later, the files that actually break a Linux container when they carry
# CRLF are still pinned.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf

# Belt and braces: the Dockerfile also runs `sed -i 's/\r$//'` over the entrypoint, so a ZIP
# download or a checkout without these attributes still boots.

# Keep the vendored minified assets byte-identical to upstream so the licence NOTICE claim
# ("unmodified from upstream") stays true and a checksum against the published file matches.
src/static/vendor/*.css -text
src/static/vendor/*.js -text

# The organizer files are inputs we must not alter in any way, including line endings.
acceptance/** -text

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
